          "def-em-gauge-transformation"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "post-qft-qed-local-coupling": "physical-assumption",
          "def-qft-framework-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-qft-qed-physical-gauge-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-qft-qed-gauge-data": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-qft-dirac-projections": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-qed-dirac-gauge-current": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-em-vacuum-maxwell": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-em-gauge-transformation": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "physical_scope": "The smooth local classical Maxwell–Dirac branch on an open flat Minkowski domain, with compactly supported variations and the stated SI signs; it does not assert a global coupled PDE solution, an interacting quantum representation, or a pointwise quantum current.",
        "empirical_premises": [],
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] for the selected free-field\ncorrespondence in [[post-qft-qed-local-coupling]]. Let $U$ be an open subset\nof flat Minkowski spacetime with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and\nlength coordinates $x^0=ct$. Let $A_{{\\rm SI},a}:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be a supplied\n$C^\\infty$ covector potential, let\n$\\psi:U\\to\\mathbb C^4$ be a $C^\\infty$ commuting spinor, and take a selected\nreal charge $q_{\\rm SI}$ and mass $m_{\\rm SI}>0$ with the conventions of\n[[def-qft-qed-physical-gauge-quantities]] and\n[[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]]. Use independent compactly supported smooth\nvariations of $A_{\\rm SI}$ and of both real and imaginary components of $\\psi$. If $(A_{\\rm SI},\\psi)$ is stationary\nfor the adopted classical action, then\n\n$$\\bigl(i\\gamma^aD_a-\\mu\\bigr)\\psi=0,\\qquad \\partial_aF_{\\rm SI}^{ab}=-\\mu_0j^b,\\qquad \\partial_aj^a=0,$$\n\nwhere $D_a=\\partial_a-iq_{\\rm SI}A_{{\\rm SI},a}/\\hbar$,\n$\\mu=m_{\\rm SI}c/\\hbar$, and\n$j^a=q_{\\rm SI}c\\,\\bar\\psi\\gamma^a\\psi$. In laboratory components this\ncurrent is $\\rho_q=q_{\\rm SI}\\psi^\\dagger\\psi$ and\n$\\mathbf J_q=q_{\\rm SI}c\\,\\psi^\\dagger\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\psi$, so\n$j=(c\\rho_q,\\mathbf J_q)$. With $[\\psi]=\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$,\n$[q_{\\rm SI}]=\\mathrm C$, and $[c]=\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, the charge density\n$\\rho_q$ has unit $\\mathrm{C\\,m^{-3}}$, while $\\mathbf J_q$ and every\ncomponent of $j^a$ have unit $\\mathrm{A\\,m^{-2}}$; here $x^0=ct$, so\n$j^0=c\\rho_q$ has the same component unit as $J_q^i$. Together with $F=dA$\non the supplied potential domain, the field equation and Bianchi identity\nhave exactly the smooth SI\nMaxwell signs in [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]]. The current and spinor here are\nclassical smooth commuting fields; this statement is not a construction of a\npointwise quantum composite operator.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted full-text Chapter 6",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "§6.3.1 for the local Dirac coupling, covariant derivative, gauge transformation, and charge current. Tong uses (+---) and natural units; the conversion below uses the explicitly declared (-+++) and SI conventions."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted 2006 prepublication book draft",
              "url": "https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "§58, printed pp. 345–347, for the spinor-electrodynamics current, local U(1) transformation, covariant derivative, and Maxwell–Dirac formulation. The source uses Heaviside–Lorentz units and c=ℏ=1; signs and units are translated explicitly."
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-qft-qed-coulomb-constraints",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Periodic neutral classical Coulomb constraint model",
        "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/workers/qed/proofs.md#Q2",
        "design_state": "Derives the periodic neutral Coulomb branch and the separate finite-mode classical first-order constraints conditionally.",
        "dependency_level": 10,
        "deps": [
          "post-qft-qed-local-coupling",
          "pthm-qft-qed-classical-coupling",
          "def-qft-qed-physical-gauge-quantities",
          "def-qft-framework-units",
          "def-qft-qed-gauge-data",
          "def-qft-dirac-projections",
          "lem-qft-qed-coulomb-constraint",
          "lem-qft-qed-dirac-first-order-constraints",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-em-si-conventions",
          "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor",
          "post-em-vacuum-maxwell"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "post-qft-qed-local-coupling": "physical-assumption",
          "pthm-qft-qed-classical-coupling": "physical-result",
          "def-qft-qed-physical-gauge-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-qft-framework-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-qft-qed-gauge-data": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-qft-dirac-projections": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-qed-coulomb-constraint": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-qed-dirac-first-order-constraints": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-em-vacuum-maxwell": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "physical_scope": "The explicit smooth, periodic, neutral Gauss branch on a flat three-torus, with a zero-mean Coulomb potential, plus the separate finite-mode classical commuting-spinor primary-constraint calculation; no nonneutral periodic solution, continuum quantum density product, or CAR derivation is asserted.",
        "empirical_premises": [],
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] for the torus Fourier\nconstruction. Let $L>0$, let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open time\ninterval, and let the spatial domain be the flat torus\n$\\mathbb T_L^3=(\\mathbb R/L\\mathbb Z)^3$, where $L$ is a positive length\nin metres. Coordinates use $x^0=ct$ with $t$ in seconds under\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. Use the smooth classical branch of\n[[post-qft-qed-local-coupling]] with smooth periodic electromagnetic\npotentials and spinor. Here $\\psi$ has the canonical length dimension\n$\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$ of [[def-qft-framework-units]], so the scalar-potential\nsource $\\rho_q:=q_{\\rm SI}\\psi^\\dagger\\psi$ has unit $\\mathrm{C,m^{-3}}$.\nOn stationary solutions it agrees\nwith the current density in [[pthm-qft-qed-classical-coupling]]. Use the\ngamma-matrix and\n$\\alpha,\\beta$ conventions of [[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]]. In Coulomb gauge\n$\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf A=0$, the multiplier equation for any smooth\nperiodic configuration is\n$\\operatorname{div}\\boldsymbol\\pi_A+\\rho_q=0$. On the explicitly neutral\nbranch\n$\\int_{\\mathbb T_L^3}\\rho_q(\\mathbf x,t)\\,d^3\\mathbf x=0$ for every $t\\in I$,\nthe zero-mean scalar potential is the unique smooth periodic solution of\n$-\\Delta\\varphi=\\rho_q/\\varepsilon_0$. With\n$\\widehat\\rho_q(\\mathbf n,t)=L^{-3}\\int_{\\mathbb T_L^3}\n\\rho_q(\\mathbf x,t)e^{-2\\pi i\\mathbf n\\cdot\\mathbf x/L}\\,d^3\\mathbf x$ and\n$\\boldsymbol\\kappa_{\\mathbf n}=2\\pi\\mathbf n/L$, the longitudinal energy is\n\n$$\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2}\\int_{\\mathbb T_L^3}|\\nabla\\varphi|^2\\,d^3\\mathbf x =\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb T_L^3}\\rho_q\\varphi\\,d^3\\mathbf x =\\frac{L^3}{2\\varepsilon_0} \\sum_{\\mathbf n\\in\\mathbb Z^3\\setminus\\{0\\}} \\frac{|\\widehat\\rho_q(\\mathbf n,t)|^2}{|\\boldsymbol\\kappa_{\\mathbf n}|^2} \\ge0.$$\n\nThe corresponding classical Hamiltonian, before imposing the Gauss\nconstraint, contains\n\n$$H=\\int_{\\mathbb T_L^3}\\left[ \\frac{|\\boldsymbol\\pi_A|^2}{2\\varepsilon_0} +\\frac{|\\mathbf B|^2}{2\\mu_0} +\\psi^\\dagger\\!\\left(c\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot (-i\\hbar\\nabla-q_{\\rm SI}\\mathbf A)+\\beta m_{\\rm SI}c^2\\right)\\!\\psi +\\varphi(\\operatorname{div}\\boldsymbol\\pi_A+\\rho_q) \\right]d^3\\mathbf x,$$\n\nwith $\\pi_\\varphi=0$ the primary multiplier momentum. In a separate finite\nmode truncation, fix $N\\ge1$ time-independent modes\n$u_1,\\ldots,u_N\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb T_L^3;\\mathbb C^4)$ satisfying\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb T_L^3}u_j(\\mathbf x)^\\dagger u_k(\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x=\\delta_{jk}.$$\nRestrict the commuting spinor field to\n$\\psi_N(\\mathbf x,t)=\\sum_{j=1}^Nz_j(t)u_j(\\mathbf x)$, where\n$z_j\\in C^\\infty(I;\\mathbb C)$ and $\\bar z_j=\\overline{z_j}$. Under the\ncanonical field normalization $[\\psi]=\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$, these modes have\nunit $\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$ and the amplitudes $z_j$ are dimensionless. This is\nonly a kinematic restriction of field configurations; the mode subspace is\nnot asserted invariant under the field equations or to contain a solution\non the neutral branch.\n\nFor its finite-dimensional canonical phase space\n$\\mathcal P_N=\\mathbb R^{4N}$, use real coordinates\n$(x_j,y_j,P^x_j,P^y_j)_{j=1}^N$ and, for $F,G\\in C^1(\\mathcal P_N;\\mathbb C)$,\nthe Poisson bracket\n$$\\{F,G\\}=\\sum_{j=1}^N\\left(\\frac{\\partial F}{\\partial x_j}\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial P^x_j}-\\frac{\\partial F}{\\partial P^x_j}\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial x_j}+\\frac{\\partial F}{\\partial y_j}\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial P^y_j}-\\frac{\\partial F}{\\partial P^y_j}\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial y_j}\\right),$$\nextended complex-bilinearly. Define\n$z_j=x_j+iy_j$, $\\bar z_j=x_j-iy_j$,\n$p_j=(P^x_j-iP^y_j)/2$, and\n$\\bar p_j=(P^x_j+iP^y_j)/2$. These definitions give\n$\\{z_j,p_k\\}=\\delta_{jk}$,\n$\\{\\bar z_j,\\bar p_k\\}=\\delta_{jk}$, and all other elementary brackets\nzero. Substitution into the adopted action gives the velocity-dependent\npart of the finite-mode physical Lagrangian\n$$L_{\\rm kin}=\\frac{i\\hbar}{2}\\sum_{j=1}^N(\\bar z_j\\dot z_j-\\dot{\\bar z}_jz_j).$$\nIts primary constraints are\n$C_j=p_j-i\\hbar\\bar z_j/2$ and\n$\\bar C_j=\\bar p_j+i\\hbar z_j/2$. For $\\hbar>0$ their constraint matrix is\n$$M=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\hbar I_N\\\\i\\hbar I_N&0\\end{pmatrix},$$\nwhich is invertible; the reduced bracket satisfies\n$\\{z_j,\\bar z_k\\}_D=-i\\delta_{jk}/\\hbar$ and\n$\\{z_j,z_k\\}_D=\\{\\bar z_j,\\bar z_k\\}_D=0$. This finite-dimensional\nclassical calculation does not derive fermionic CAR. The neutral torus\nbranch is an explicit hypothesis.\nFor the selected electron, $q_{\\rm SI}=-e_{\\rm SI}\\ne0$, so a neutral\nsingle-species commuting-spinor configuration has $\\psi=0$; no nontrivial\nneutral charged classical Dirac configuration is asserted. The finite-mode\nconstraint calculation is separate and does not claim to be a solution in\nthat branch.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted full-text Chapter 6, §§6.2–6.3",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "§6.2 for the Maxwell multiplier and Gauss constraint; §6.3.1 for the Dirac–Maxwell coupling. The source uses natural units and nonperiodic spatial boundary conditions, so the torus solver is proved conditionally by the local mathematical supplier."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted 2006 prepublication book draft",
              "url": "https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "§54, printed pp. 332–334, for Maxwell's equations and potential convention; §55, printed pp. 335–338, for the scalar-potential constraint, Coulomb Hamiltonian, and transverse field. Its spatial-infinity boundary condition is not substituted for the periodic neutral hypothesis here."
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      }
    ]
